\date{2026-02-14T16:54:31Z}
\title{Thoughts on pedagogy and community-building}
\author{brentyorgey}

\p{This semester I am participating in a "teaching square" with several colleagues, where we visit and observe each other's classes—not for the purpose of evaluation, or even constructive criticism, but merely to observe and reflect on our own teaching. On Monday I visited a class taught by one of my colleagues (who shall remain anonymous for now since I don't know whether she would want to be identified publicly in this context).  I very much enjoyed watching the way my colleague got the students to engage actively with her and with each other.  It seemed like the students in the class all knew each others' names, which in my experience is rare, even in a small class.  I don't think this was a coincidence—it was something my colleague intentionally cultivated.}

\p{This week I also read [[shriramkrishnamurthi]]'s [pedagogy recommendations](https://parentheticallyspeaking.org/articles/pedagogy-recommendations/), and among other good advice, he also says several things about creating community within the classroom (such as getting students to introduce themselves to each other repeatedly).}

\p{I'm still thinking about what, if anything, I might concretely do differently in my classes, but it's been good to think about.}
